1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,560
In January 2009,

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:07,680
Zimbabwe's central bank issued a single
banknote worth one hundred trillion

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:12,480
dollars. That's a one,
followed by fourteen zeros.

4
00:00:12,520 --> 00:00:16,560
On its first day, it was
worth about thirty US dollars.

5
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:21,680
A few months earlier, prices in Zimbabwe
were doubling roughly every day.

6
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:26,240
The economist Steve Hanke
estimated that in November 2008,

7
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:32,000
inflation hit seventy-nine point six
billion percent in a single month.

8
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:37,493
Today, walk into a shop in Harare and
you'll most likely pay in US dollars.

9
00:00:37,547 --> 00:00:42,000
A survey by the Confederation of Zimbabwe
Industries found that seventy-seven

10
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:47,760
percent of business sales in the
first half of 2026 were in US dollars.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,520
Yet Zimbabwe has its own
currency again, called ZiG.

12
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:57,240
So why does a country with its own
money still run on someone else's?

13
00:00:57,280 --> 00:01:02,200
Let's find out. Picture a
market in Harare in 2008.

14
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:04,920
A loaf of bread has a
price in the morning.

15
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:08,360
By the afternoon,
that price is out of date.

16
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,000
When prices double roughly every day,

17
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:15,880
a salary paid on Friday can lose much
of its value by the following week.

18
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:19,653
People carried bags of
cash just to buy groceries.

19
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:24,200
And when money loses value that fast,
everyone does the same thing.

20
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:26,440
You spend it the moment you get it,

21
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:29,800
or you swap it for something
that holds its value.

22
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:34,640
For many families, that meant US
dollars and South African rand.

23
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,387
Why did it happen? Put simply,

24
00:01:37,413 --> 00:01:41,400
when a government prints money much
faster than the economy makes bread,

25
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:45,440
fuel and everything else,
each note buys less.

26
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:48,613
More money chasing the same loaf of bread.

27
00:01:48,667 --> 00:01:52,960
And once people expect prices
to rise, they spend even faster,

28
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:55,160
which pushes prices up again.

29
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,120
The central bank kept
printing bigger notes.

30
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:01,240
Millions. Billions. Trillions.

31
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:06,320
Then, in January 2009,
the hundred trillion dollar note.

32
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:08,200
It still wasn't enough.

33
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:13,880
A note with fourteen zeros could not keep
up with prices that doubled every day.

34
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:19,480
At the end of January 2009, the acting
finance minister, Patrick Chinamasa,

35
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:21,600
read the budget in parliament.

36
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:26,840
In it, he allowed people to use other
currencies alongside the Zimbabwe dollar.

37
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:31,800
The list included the US dollar,
the South African rand, the Botswana pula,

38
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:34,360
the euro and the British pound.

39
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:38,760
Almost overnight, the US dollar
became the main currency.

40
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:43,280
In April 2009, the Zimbabwe
dollar was suspended.

41
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:47,067
Using another country's money
is called dollarisation,

42
00:02:47,093 --> 00:02:49,560
and Zimbabwe wasn't the first to do it.

43
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:55,040
Ecuador adopted the US dollar in
2000, and El Salvador in 2001.

44
00:02:55,120 --> 00:03:00,000
Here's why it works. When your money
is the same money used in America,

45
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:03,080
your own central bank
can't print more of it.

46
00:03:03,360 --> 00:03:05,840
So the runaway prices stopped.

47
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:10,000
A price in the morning was still
the same price in the afternoon.

48
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:14,840
In 2015, the government officially
closed the old currency.

49
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:20,400
Bank balances were swapped at a rate of
thirty-five quadrillion Zimbabwe dollars for

50
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:25,560
one US dollar. Anyone with up to one
hundred and seventy-five quadrillion in the

51
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:28,880
bank got a flat five US dollars.

52
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:30,960
And that hundred trillion dollar note?

53
00:03:31,200 --> 00:03:35,280
By 2015, it was worth
about forty US cents.

54
00:03:36,153 --> 00:03:39,313
But using someone else's
money has a catch.

55
00:03:39,593 --> 00:03:44,233
You can only spend the dollars that
come into the country: from exports,

56
00:03:44,313 --> 00:03:47,753
from investors, and from
family working abroad.

57
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:51,513
And you can't print more
when cash runs short.

58
00:03:51,593 --> 00:03:55,353
By 2016, cash was running short.

59
00:03:55,433 --> 00:03:59,673
When the central bank introduced
bond notes in November 2016,

60
00:03:59,753 --> 00:04:03,433
banks limited how much
cash people could withdraw.

61
00:04:03,513 --> 00:04:07,913
The bond notes were meant to be
worth exactly one US dollar each,

62
00:04:07,993 --> 00:04:11,913
backed by a loan from the
African Export-Import Bank.

63
00:04:12,193 --> 00:04:16,753
Then, in February 2019,
came a new electronic currency,

64
00:04:16,793 --> 00:04:21,073
the RTGS dollar. In June 2019,

65
00:04:21,113 --> 00:04:25,753
the government renamed it the Zimbabwe
dollar and banned foreign currencies for

66
00:04:25,833 --> 00:04:28,993
everyday trade. It didn't last.

67
00:04:29,033 --> 00:04:34,526
By December 2019, annual inflation
was above five hundred percent.

68
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:39,033
And in March 2020, the US
dollar was allowed back.

69
00:04:39,113 --> 00:04:42,993
Through all of this, one thing
kept many families going:

70
00:04:43,033 --> 00:04:46,686
support from relatives
working outside the country.

71
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:49,153
And for families with
someone in South Africa,

72
00:04:49,193 --> 00:04:52,393
that's exactly what we
built Fluxr for.

